{
  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (RNA-only, single profile, PCA n2000) achieves 48.5% accuracy, while config_010 (image-only, ResNet50) is much weaker at 20.8%, confirming RNA is the dominant signal. However, we have only one RNA data point, so we cannot tell whether PCA (a simple linear projection) is near-optimal or whether a learned nonlinear encoder would substantially improve representation quality. Question: config_004 (RNA-only, single profile, scvi_n200) changes only the RNA encoder from PCA to scvi while holding profile setting and all other factors constant, giving a clean A/B comparison of representation quality. Why this is the best next experiment: With only 2 completed runs, the highest-uncertainty design axis is whether the RNA encoder choice matters. If scvi significantly outperforms PCA, it justifies investing remaining budget in learned encoders (scvi, scgpt) and their multiprofile variants; if not, PCA suffices and we should pivot to testing aggregation/multiprofile or fusion strategies. This single experiment maximally reduces uncertainty about the most fundamental representation decision before committing budget to more complex multimodal or multiprofile configurations."
}

